\section{Introduction}

This document presents a complete BitTorrent client implemented as a literate
program in Rust. The goal is twofold: to explain the BitTorrent protocol from
first principles, and to demonstrate how literate programming can make complex
network protocols accessible. By the end, you will understand not just how
BitTorrent works, but why it was designed the way it was.

\subsection{What is BitTorrent?}

BitTorrent is a peer-to-peer file sharing protocol designed for efficient
distribution of large files across the Internet. Unlike traditional downloads
where a single server sends data to many clients, BitTorrent distributes both
the content and the bandwidth burden across all participants.

\paragraph{The Problem with Traditional Downloads.}
Consider a scenario where a software vendor releases a 4 GB update and a
million users want to download it simultaneously. With a traditional
client-server model, the vendor's server must send 4 petabytes of data---the
same bytes, over and over, to each client. The server becomes the bottleneck:
as more users arrive, everyone's download slows to a crawl. This is
fundamentally inefficient because each user downloads data and then does
nothing with it.

\paragraph{The BitTorrent Solution.}
BitTorrent inverts this model. Instead of one server feeding many clients,
every participant both downloads \emph{and uploads}. When you download a file
via BitTorrent, you simultaneously upload the pieces you've already received
to other users who need them. The more popular a file becomes, the more
bandwidth becomes available to distribute it---the exact opposite of the
traditional model.

\paragraph{Key Concepts.}
To understand BitTorrent, you need to grasp four fundamental concepts:

\begin{description}
\item[Pieces] Files are divided into fixed-size chunks called \emph{pieces},
  typically 256 KB to 4 MB each. Each piece is identified by its SHA1 hash
  (Secure Hash Algorithm 1, a cryptographic hash producing a 20-byte fingerprint),
  which serves both as verification (ensuring data integrity) and as a unique
  identifier (so peers can request specific pieces).

\item[Swarms] All the peers sharing a particular torrent form a \emph{swarm}.
  Within a swarm, every peer can potentially connect to every other peer.
  The swarm is the fundamental unit of coordination---there is no central
  server distributing data, only peers exchanging pieces with each other.

\item[Seeders and Leechers] A peer who has the complete file is a
  \emph{seeder}---they only upload, never download. A peer still downloading
  is a \emph{leecher}. Healthy torrents have a good ratio of seeders to
  leechers. When you finish downloading, you become a seeder yourself (unless
  you disconnect), contributing back to the swarm.

\item[Trackers] While peers exchange data directly, they need a way to find
  each other. A \emph{tracker} is a server that maintains a list of peers for
  each torrent. When you start a download, your client contacts the tracker
  to get a list of IP addresses to connect to. The tracker never sees the
  actual content---it only facilitates introductions.
\end{description}

\paragraph{Why BitTorrent Succeeds.}
Several design choices make BitTorrent remarkably effective:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{No single point of failure.} If one peer leaves, others continue
  sharing. If the tracker goes down temporarily, already-connected peers can
  continue exchanging data.

\item \textbf{Scalability through participation.} Each new peer brings
  bandwidth to the swarm. Popular content becomes faster to download, not
  slower.

\item \textbf{Incentive alignment.} The ``tit-for-tat'' algorithm preferentially
  uploads to peers who upload back. This discourages free-riding and encourages
  contribution.

\item \textbf{Verification built-in.} Every piece is verified against its hash
  before being accepted. Corrupted or malicious data is detected and discarded
  automatically.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{How BitTorrent Works}

Before diving into code, let's trace the lifecycle of a typical BitTorrent
