\paragraph{Step 1: Obtain and Parse the Torrent File.}
Everything starts with a \texttt{.torrent} file (or a magnet link, which we
won't cover here). This small file, typically a few kilobytes, contains:
\begin{itemize}
\item The tracker URL(s) where peers can be found
\item Metadata about the file(s): names, sizes, directory structure
\item The piece length and SHA1 hashes of every piece
\end{itemize}
The torrent file uses a simple encoding called \emph{bencode}. Parsing this
file is our first task.

\paragraph{Step 2: Announce to the Tracker.}
With the torrent parsed, we contact the tracker to announce ourselves. Our
request includes:
\begin{itemize}
\item The \emph{info hash}: a SHA1 hash of the torrent's metadata, uniquely
  identifying this content
\item Our \emph{peer ID}: a unique identifier for our client
\item Our IP address and port where we'll accept connections
\item How much we've downloaded/uploaded so far
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Step 3: Receive the Peer List.}
The tracker responds with a list of other peers in the swarm---their IP
addresses and ports. We now know who to talk to, but we haven't yet
connected to anyone or downloaded any data.

\paragraph{Step 4: Connect to Peers and Exchange Pieces.}
This is where the real work happens. For each peer:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Establish a TCP connection and perform a \emph{handshake} to verify
  we're both interested in the same torrent
\item Exchange \emph{bitfields}---bitmaps indicating which pieces each side has
\item Express \emph{interest} in pieces the peer has that we don't
\item Request specific \emph{blocks} (sub-piece units, typically 16 KB)
\item Receive blocks, assemble them into pieces, and verify SHA1 hashes
\item Upload pieces we have to peers who request them
\end{enumerate}

This continues until we have all pieces. At that point, we become a seeder,
uploading to others without needing to download anything ourselves.

\subsection{Document Roadmap}

This document is organized to follow the logical structure of the protocol,
building from simple foundations to the complete client. Each chapter adds
one layer of functionality, and the code chunks accumulate into a working
implementation.

\begin{description}
\item[Chapter 1: Bencode] introduces the encoding format used throughout
  BitTorrent. Bencode is simple---just integers, strings, lists, and
  dictionaries---but its canonical encoding property is essential for
  computing consistent info hashes. We implement a complete encoder and
  decoder.

\item[Chapter 2: Metainfo Files] builds on bencode to parse \texttt{.torrent}
  files. We extract tracker URLs, file metadata, piece lengths, and piece
  hashes. The crucial info hash computation happens here.

\item[Chapter 3: Tracker Protocol] implements HTTP tracker communication.
  We construct announce requests, parse responses, and handle both compact
  and dictionary-format peer lists. Error handling covers network failures,
  tracker rejections, and malformed responses.

\item[Chapter 4: Peer Wire Protocol] defines the messages peers exchange:
  handshakes, bitfields, interested/choke state, and piece requests. We
  implement serialization and deserialization for all message types.

\item[Chapter 5: Piece Management] tracks download progress. The piece
  manager knows which pieces we have, which are in progress, and which are
  verified. It coordinates block requests across multiple peer connections.

\item[Chapter 6: Command-Line Interface] provides the user-facing entry
  point. We use Rust's \texttt{clap} crate to parse arguments and configure
  the download: torrent file, output directory, port, and verbosity.

\item[Chapter 7: The Client] brings everything together. The client struct
  owns all state and orchestrates the download: tracker announces, peer
  connections, piece assembly, and progress reporting.

\item[Chapter 8: Disk I/O] handles file creation and storage. We create
  sparse files for efficient disk usage and write verified pieces to the
  correct offsets within the file structure.

\item[Appendix] provides references to the official BitTorrent Enhancement
  Proposals (BEPs) and other resources for further reading.
\end{description}

By following these chapters in order, you will build a complete understanding
of the BitTorrent protocol and a working client implementation. The literate
programming format means you can read this as documentation, study the code
in context, or extract the implementation to compile and run.

